.TH HUSH 1 "" "hush" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hush \- a trinary notifications filter, placement, and tray icon for mako
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dnd-comms-toggle
.RB [ status | toggle | "set " STATE | render ]
.br
.B mako-placement
.br
.B comms-indicator
.RB [ render-test " " DIR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
hush is a small notifications suite over
.BR mako (1).
There is no eponymous
.B hush
command; the three tools below are invoked by name (the tray icon runs as a
.B --user
service).
.PP
The FILTER is trinary:
.B all
hides nothing;
.B work
hides everything except the ALLOWLIST and the WORK SET;
.B none
hides everything except the allowlist. The allowlist is what is never
personal comms (a battery warning, an agent's alert, anything critical), so
it flows in every state.
.PP
The work set comes from one of two places. In
.B apps
mode (the default) it is a hand-kept list of app names. In
.B origin
mode it is whatever a configured command prints: where work apps run behind
a relay that stamps every notification with its origin as the app name, work
is decided by where a notification CAME FROM, so no app list is kept and the
same app on both sides is classified correctly. hush knows nothing about
what answers the command; an integrator names it.
.PP
If the work set cannot be known (the command fails or prints nothing),
.B work
hides NOTHING and says so: on stderr, in
.BR "setup.sh check" ,
and as a badge on the tray icon. A silently lost work notification is the
worse failure.
.SH TOOLS
.TP
.B dnd-comms-toggle
The filter state machine.
.B toggle
cycles all, work, none;
.BI "set " state
jumps to one; a bare call (or
.BR status )
prints the state.
Every change re-renders the filter first, so the work set is current.
.B render
re-renders it alone (and reloads a running mako): an integrator runs it
when the work set changes. It drives mako via
.BR makoctl " mode."
State lives in
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/dnd-comms-state .
.TP
.B mako-placement
Writes the notification placement/sizing to mako's
.I placement.active
include for the current display. It reads the per-shape
.I ~/.config/shapes/<shape>/mako.conf
and the display density when a shape resolver (hwdp) is present, and
falls back to a shipped single-output default otherwise. Both are OPTIONAL soft
dependencies; hush degrades cleanly without them.
.TP
.B comms-indicator
An SNI tray icon drawn as an owned glyph (a rounded tile, frame colour = state).
It reads the filter state to draw and left-click cycles it via
.BR dnd-comms-toggle .
A white
.B !
badge, and a line in the tooltip, mean the filter could not do what its
state promises.
Runs as the
.I comms-indicator.service
.B --user
unit (a venv-backed Python daemon; needs a tray host).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I ~/.config/hush/config
(else the shipped
.IR share/hush/config ,
which it REPLACES rather than merges with): one
.IB key = value
per line,
.B #
comments, list keys repeated once per entry. Names are exact mako app names.
.TP
.BR work-from= apps | origin
where the work set comes from (default
.BR apps ).
.TP
.BI work-app= name
(apps) an app that is work.
.TP
.BI origin-command= command
(origin) run with
.BR sh ;
the first field of each output line is a work app name. A non-zero exit means
the list is not trusted.
.TP
.BI allow= name
an app that flows in every state.
.TP
.BR allow-critical= yes | no
a critical-urgency notification flows in every state.
.PP
An unknown key, or a name with a quote or backslash (which would break mako's
WHOLE config), keeps the last good filter and is reported like an unknown work
set.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/mako/config
hush's mako config (appearance + layout); include's placement.active and
filter.active.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mako/filter.active
The generated filter. Do not edit; it is rewritten on every change.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/dnd-comms-warn
What the filter could not do, if anything; read by the tray icon.
.TP
.I ~/.config/shapes/<shape>/mako.conf
Optional per-shape placement/sizing (a shared, integrator-owned tree); read when
present.
.SH RUNTIME DEPENDENCIES
.BR mako "(1), " makoctl "(1); a StatusNotifierItem tray host for the icon."
Optional: a display-shape resolver (hwdp) for per-shape sizing.
.SH SETUP
.B ./setup.sh install
copies the suite to ~/.local/share/hush and links its commands,
man page and mako config into it;
.B ./setup.sh service
builds the tray-icon venv and enables its daemon;
.B ./setup.sh check
audits the install. See the README.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mako (1),
.BR makoctl (1)
